Table: Automatic Data Mapping
What makes this Table special?
In ezui, the Table is “Smart.” You don’t have to tell it what the headers are or manually type every row. Instead, you give it a List of Objects, and the Table handles the rest.
Step 1: The ITable Contract
To show your data in a table, your class (like Student or Book) must “implement” the ITable interface. This tells the table how to “read” your object.
public class Student implements ITable {
    private String id, name, grade;

    // The Table calls this to build the top header row
    public String[] getHeaders() { 
        return new String[]{"ID", "Student Name", "Current Grade"}; 
    }

    // The Table calls this to fill in each row
    public String[] getValues() { 
        return new String[]{id, name, grade}; 
    }
}

Code Anatomy - Handling Multiple Errors
try {
    String input = scanner.nextLine();
    int num = Integer.parseInt(input); // Potential NumberFormatException
    System.out.println(10 / num);      // Potential ArithmeticException
} catch (NumberFormatException e) {
    System.out.println("Error: Please enter a valid integer.");
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Error: Cannot divide by zero.");
} catch (Exception e) {
    System.out.println("Something else went wrong: " + e.getMessage());
} finally {
    System.out.println("Cleanup code can go here.");
}

Pro-Tip: Always catch specific exceptions first. If you catch the general Exception class first, the more specific ones below it will never be reached.

Defining and Throwing Your Own Exceptions

You don’t have to wait for the JVM to find an error. You can trigger one yourself to enforce rules.
The throw Keyword:

public void setAge(int age) {
    if (age &lt; 0) {
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Age cannot be negative!");
    }
    this.age = age;
}

This is “Defensive Programming”—stopping bad data before it ruins your program’s state.

Exam Prep - “What is the Output?”
Question: What prints if the user enters “0”?
try {
    int x = 5 / Integer.parseInt(input);
    System.out.println("A");
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("B");
} finally {
    System.out.println("C");
}

Answer: B then C. “A” is skipped because the error happens on the first line of the try.

Threads in Java — Option 1: Extend Thread
class MyThread extends Thread {
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Hello from " + getName());
    }
}

Subclass Thread and override run() with the work you want done.
Start it with new MyThread().start().
Works, but it ties your class to Thread — your class can no longer extend anything else, and “the work” and “how it’s run” are welded together.


Timing: ~2 min. Read the code aloud line by line — don’t assume the syntax is self-explanatory.

Threads in Java — Option 2: Implement Runnable (preferred)
Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    System.out.println("Hello from lambda");
});
t.start();
t.join(); // wait for t to finish

Pass the work to new Thread(...) instead of subclassing. Your code stays independent of how it’s run.
() -&gt; { ... } is a lambda — shorthand for “here is a block of code, treat it as a value.” Read it as: “a nameless block of code that, when run, prints the message.”
Handing that block to new Thread(...) says “run this on a new thread.”


Timing: ~2 min.

Threads in Java — The Four Methods
Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    System.out.println("Hello from lambda");
});
t.start();
t.join(); // wait for t to finish

new Thread(...) — creates the thread object. Nothing is running yet.
start() — actually launches the new thread. This is the only line that creates concurrency.
join() — makes the calling thread wait until this one finishes, before continuing.
run() — the code the thread executes. Never call run() directly — the next slide shows exactly why.


Timing: ~2 min. The lambda gloss on the previous slide carries the explanation; still worth restating verbally in your own words.

Tracing It: What Actually Happens
Thread t = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    System.out.println("Hello from lambda");
});
t.start();
t.join();
System.out.println("Main thread continues");
Five steps. What does each thread do at each one? (next slide)

Timing: ~1 min. Read the code, then have students predict the order of the two printed lines before you show the table.

The Thread Lifecycle — How a Thread Moves Through It

Every thread you create moves through these states.

new Thread(...) puts it in New;
start() moves it to Runnable;
it becomes Running only when it actually gets to execute;
and it can bounce between Running and Blocked/Waiting many times before reaching Terminated.


Timing: ~2 min. Trace the diagram left to right against the table on the previous slide, state by state.

Integer.parseInt — Numbers from the User

IO.readln always produces a String — even if the user types 42, the program receives the text “42”, not the number 42. That distinction matters because you cannot do arithmetic on text. To convert “42” to the number 42, use Integer.parseInt. The syntax is straightforward: wrap the String in Integer.parseInt(…). Mention that if the user types something that is not a valid number — like “hello” — the program will crash. Handling that gracefully is a topic for later in the course.

IO.readln always produces a String — even if the user types a number.
String input = IO.readln("How many minutes? ");
// input holds "137" — the TEXT, not the number
You cannot do arithmetic on a String:
input / 60    // ERROR — can't divide text
Integer.parseInt converts a String to an int:
int minutes = Integer.parseInt(input);
// now minutes holds the number 137
Or in one step:
int minutes = Integer.parseInt(IO.readln("How many minutes? "));






Warning


If the user types something that is not a whole number — like "hello" or "3.5" — the program will crash. Handling that gracefully is a topic for later in the course.

String Concatenation

This is the formal introduction of string concatenation, deferred from the variables deck. The + operator does two different things depending on the types involved. int + int is arithmetic. String + anything is concatenation — the values are joined into a single String. The anything part is important: Java automatically converts a number to its text form before joining. Show the clean cases first, then the surprising mixed case. The left-to-right evaluation rule is the key to understanding “5” + 3 + 4 = “534” vs 5 + 3 + “4” = “84”. Connect back to deck 02: we printed labels and values on separate lines because we did not have concatenation yet — now we can put them on the same line.

The + operator does different things depending on the types involved.
With two numbers — addition:
int a = 5;
int b = 3;
IO.println(a + b);     // prints: 8
With a String — concatenation (joining):
String first = "Hello, ";
String second = "world!";
IO.println(first + second);    // prints: Hello, world!
Mixing a String and a number:
int score = 42;
IO.println("Score: " + score);   // prints: Score: 42
Java converts the number to text automatically before joining.

