Part 1 — Observe a Race Condition (20 min)
Copy and run the following Java program three times. Record the output each time.
public class RaceCondition {
    static int counter = 0;

    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        Thread t1 = new Thread(() -&gt; {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10000; i++) counter++;
        });
        Thread t2 = new Thread(() -&gt; {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10000; i++) counter++;
        });
        t1.start();
        t2.start();
        t1.join();
        t2.join();
        System.out.println("Final counter: " + counter);
    }
}
Questions — answer in a text file or on paper:

What value did you expect the counter to be? What values did you actually get across your three runs?
Why does the counter not always reach the expected value?
Two threads are both doing counter++. Explain in your own words what three low-level steps counter++ actually involves and why that makes it unsafe.

Part 2 — Fix It with synchronized (20 min)
Modify the program so that the counter always reaches the correct value. Add the synchronized keyword to a helper method and call it instead of counter++ directly.
// Replace the counter++ lines with a call to this method:
static synchronized void increment() {
    counter++;
}
Run the fixed version three times. Confirm the output is always correct.
Questions:

What value does the counter reach now on every run?
In one sentence: what does synchronized prevent?
Do you notice any difference in how long the program takes to run? Why might synchronized slow things down?

Indentation Is the Syntax


Java
if (score &gt;= 60) {
    System.out.println("Pass");
} else {
    System.out.println("Fail");
}

Python
if score &gt;= 60:
    print("Pass")
else:
    print("Fail")


No { } braces, no ; semicolons — a colon : opens a block, and indentation (consistent spaces) marks what’s inside it
Mixing tabs and spaces, or indenting inconsistently, is a real error in Python — it isn’t just a style preference like in Java
Colab auto-indents for you after a colon — trust it, don’t fight it


~3 min. This trips up Java developers hard in Week 1 — an accidentally-misaligned line is a silent bug, not a compiler error shouting at you. Emphasize it strongly.

For Loops, Side by Side


Java
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

Python
for i in range(5):
    print(i)


Java’s for-loop spells out three separate things: start, condition, increment
Python’s range(5) bundles all three into one call: produces 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 — five values, not including 5
range(start, stop) and range(start, stop, step) both work, same off-by-one convention: stop is never included


~3 min. The “stop is excluded” rule is the single most common off-by-one bug source this semester — call it out explicitly and have students predict range(2, 8)’s output before revealing it.

Looping Over Data Directly


Java
int[] scores = {88, 92, 75};
for (int s : scores) {
    System.out.println(s);
}

Python
scores = [88, 92, 75]
for s in scores:
    print(s)


Java’s enhanced for-loop and Python’s for x in list do the same thing: hand you each element in turn, no index needed
Need the index too? for i, s in enumerate(scores): gives you both — closest Python equivalent to a classic indexed loop


~2 min. Students will reach for for i in range(len(scores)): print(scores[i]) out of habit — show it works, then show for s in scores: is the idiomatic version and gets used almost everywhere in this course.

While Loops and Conditionals


Java
int n = 10;
while (n &gt; 0) {
    n--;
}
if (n == 0 && ready) {
    n = 1;
}

Python
n = 10
while n &gt; 0:
    n -= 1

if n == 0 and ready:
    n = 1



&&, ||, ! in Java become the words and, or, not in Python
No n-- or n++ shorthand in Python — write n -= 1 / n += 1
== still means “equals” in both languages — that one didn’t change


~2 min. Quick slide, mostly a reference table students will return to — don’t over-explain, just point out the word-vs-symbol swap.

Functions, Side by Side


Java
static double average(
        int a, int b) {
    return (a + b) / 2.0;
}

Python
def average(a, b):
    return (a + b) / 2


def replaces the return-type-plus-name pattern — Python functions don’t declare a return type
No static, no access modifiers (public/private) — Python’s simplicity here is deliberate, not something being hidden
Python allows default arguments: def average(a, b=0): lets a caller omit b entirely — Java requires overloading a whole separate method for that


~3 min. Default arguments are new territory for a Java-only background — worth a concrete second example (greet(name, greeting="Hello"), called both with and without greeting).

The Console I/O You Already Know
Before diving into files, recall how you handled console I/O in CS1:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // READING from the keyboard
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        String name = sc.nextLine();
        int age = sc.nextInt();

        // WRITING to the console
        System.out.println("Hello, " + name);
        System.out.printf("Age: %d%n", age);
    }
}

The File Class
Before reading or writing, it’s useful to know about the File class. A File object represents a path — it doesn’t open or read the file, it just describes where it is.
import java.io.File;

// in the main method ..
File f = new File("students.csv");
Think of it like a sticky note with a file path written on it. It doesn’t touch the file — it just holds the address.

File in Action — Defensive File Handling
The most practical use of File is checking before you act:
import java.io.File;
import java.util.Scanner;
import java.io.FileNotFoundException;

public class SafeRead {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        File f = new File("students.csv");

        if (!f.exists()) {
            System.out.println("File not found: " + f.getAbsolutePath());
            return;                           // exit early, nothing to read
        }

        System.out.println("Reading: " + f.getName());
